  <h1>How to Evaluate an IVD Rapid Test Supplier</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Evaluating an IVD supplier means verifying certificates, documentation, stability data and OEM capability before committing to volume.</p>
  <p>A practical checklist for distributors, importers and procurement teams.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0;">1. Regulatory &amp; certification</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>CE marking (EU IVDR) — which product lines, certificate number and scope</li>
      <li>ISO 13485 QMS certificate — issuing body and validity</li>
      <li>FDA 510(k) / NMPA registration where relevant to your market</li>
      <li>Free Sale Certificate (FSC)</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0;">2. Product documentation</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>IFU (instructions for use) and datasheet per product</li>
      <li>Sensitivity / specificity / cross-reactivity validation data</li>
      <li>Stability and shelf-life data, storage conditions</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0;">3. Sample &amp; lot evaluation</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Request samples and validate against your reference method</li>
      <li>Confirm batch-to-batch (lot-to-lot) consistency</li>
      <li>Check packaging, labeling and cut-off alignment to your regulatory standard</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0;">4. OEM/ODM &amp; supply</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>MOQ and lead time</li>
      <li>Private label / custom panel capability</li>
      <li>Registration support documents (CE files, validation)</li>
      <li>Export documentation (COA, shipping, dangerous-goods where applicable)</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p><a href="/rfq/">Request samples, IFU and datasheets →</a></p>

